---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro'
import EnvTable from '../components/EnvTable.astro'
import Step from '../components/Step.astro'
import { parseEnvExample, readRepoFile } from '../lib/repo'

const compose = readRepoFile('docker/compose.yaml')
const entries = parseEnvExample(readRepoFile('docker/.env.example'))
---
<Page
  title="Install PSPad"
  description="Run PSPad on your own hardware with Docker Compose: the stack, every environment variable, the secrets, and the first sign-in."
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Install</h1>
    <p>
      Four containers — API, client, MongoDB, Keycloak — one Compose file. Budget a
      few minutes for the first build.
    </p>

    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Docker with Compose v2 (<code>docker compose version</code> prints v2.x).</li>
      <li>About 2 GB of disk.</li>
      <li>Beyond <code>localhost</code>: a reverse proxy for TLS. The stack serves plain HTTP only.</li>
      <li>
        Optional: outbound HTTPS from the API container to <code>api.nbp.pl</code>. Only a
        budget's exchange-rate lookups use it: the NBP button in the entry and holding panels
        and the Balance tab's "At NBP today" tiles. Without it the button fails, the tiles stay
        hidden and everything else works. No env var or port is involved.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <Step n={1} title="Clone the repository">
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/psp515/PSPad.git
cd PSPad</code></pre>
    </Step>

    <Step n={2} title="Create your environment file">
      <pre><code>cp docker/.env.example docker/.env</code></pre>
      <p>
        Do this first, or the stack starts with empty values. Defaults work on
        <code>localhost</code> — change <code>MONGO_PASSWORD</code> and
        <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> before it's reachable from anywhere else.
      </p>
      <EnvTable entries={entries} />
      <Callout kind="warning" title="Two Keycloak URLs">
        <p>
          <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code> is for your <strong>browser</strong>.
          <code>KEYCLOAK_INTERNAL_AUTHORITY</code> is for the <strong>API container</strong> —
          they reach Keycloak by different names, so <code>localhost</code> means something
          different to each. Running an external Keycloak? Set both to the same address.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <Callout kind="warning" title="The admin credentials apply once to Keycloak, but the API keeps reading them">
        <p>
          <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER</code> and <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> only
          bootstrap Keycloak itself on the very first start — change the password in the
          Keycloak console afterwards and editing <code>.env</code> does nothing to Keycloak.
          But the <code>api</code> container reads these same two variables at runtime, every
          time it calls Keycloak's admin API to delete a user's login on account deletion.
          Rotate the password in the Keycloak console without also updating <code>.env</code>
          and restarting <code>api</code>, and account deletion keeps wiping the user's data
          but silently stops removing their Keycloak login.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <Step n={3} title="Start the stack">
      <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
      <p>The first build takes a few minutes. Mongo starts as a replica set — required for transactions. Wait for it to report healthy:</p>
      <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml ps</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>api</code>, <code>app</code> and <code>keycloak</code> show a bare
        <code>Up</code> forever — that's normal. Confirm the API is actually answering:
      </p>
      <pre><code>curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{'{'}http_code{'}'}\n" http://localhost:5000/api/today</code></pre>
      <p><code>401</code> is correct — you're just not signed in yet. Anything else means the stack didn't come up cleanly.</p>
      <Callout title="The API runs in Production mode, on purpose">
        <p>
          It can still reach Keycloak over plain HTTP because <code>compose.yaml</code> sets
          <code>Keycloak__RequireHttpsMetadata: "false"</code> for the <code>api</code> service
          directly — not because it's in Development mode. Putting Keycloak behind HTTPS later?
          Flip that value instead of setting <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code>, which would
          also leak stack traces to anyone who can reach the API.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <Step n={4} title="Create your first user">
      <p>No registration screen — accounts live in Keycloak, and you need one before the app is usable.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open the admin console at <code>KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME</code>
          (<code>http://localhost:8080</code> by default) and sign in with
          <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER</code> / <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code>.
        </li>
        <li>Switch realm from <strong>master</strong> to <strong>psplace</strong>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Users</strong> → <strong>Create new user</strong>. Fill in username, email,
          first and last name.
        </li>
        <li>
          Then <strong>Credentials</strong> → <strong>Set password</strong>, with
          <em>Temporary</em> off — otherwise Keycloak forces a password change the client
          can't show.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </Step>

    <Step n={5} title="Sign in">
      <p>
        Open <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code>, sign in, and land on My Day — empty at first,
        while three starter areas provision in the background. Install it from your
        browser's address bar for the offline app.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="warning" title="Give it 20–30 seconds before you trust something is saved">
        <p>
          The app is offline-first: new tasks save to your browser immediately and sync to
          the server every 20–30 seconds. Restart the stack right after creating something
          and it's gone — it never made it to MongoDB.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <Callout title="Sessions are kept long on purpose">
        <p>
          The bundled realm keeps sessions for <strong>30 days idle, 90 days maximum</strong> —
          long enough that an installed PWA stays signed in for as long as the app's own
          offline trust window allows. Realm <strong>psplace</strong> is shared across every
          self-hosted application it serves, so this lifetime applies to all of them, not
          only PSPad.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <h2>The stack, in full</h2>
    <p><code>docker/compose.yaml</code>, exactly as it ships:</p>
    <pre><code>{compose}</code></pre>

    <h2>Putting it behind a proxy</h2>
    <p>
      Point three hostnames at the published ports — client <code>5001</code>, API
      <code>5000</code>, Keycloak <code>8080</code> — terminate TLS at the proxy, and forward
      <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Set <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code>, <code>API_BASE_ADDRESS</code>,
        <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code> and <code>KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME</code> in
        <code>docker/.env</code> to your public <code>https://</code> URLs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Update the Keycloak client too, or sign-in fails.</strong> The bundled realm
        ships with <code>http://localhost:5001/*</code> baked into its redirect URI — changing
        <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code> doesn't touch it. Realm <strong>psplace</strong> →
        <strong>Clients</strong> → <strong>pspad-frontend</strong> → update
        <strong>Valid redirect URIs</strong>. <strong>Web origins</strong> follows
        automatically (it's <code>+</code>, unless you've changed it).
      </li>
      <li>
        Tell the API which proxy to believe. Public snapshot links are rate-limited per visitor
        address; without this every visitor arrives from the proxy's address and they all
        share one limit. Set <code>API_TRUSTED_PROXY_NETWORK</code> in <code>docker/.env</code>
        to the address range the proxy connects from — usually the stack's Docker network:
        <pre><code>{"docker network inspect pspad_pspad --format '{{range .IPAM.Config}}{{.Subnet}}{{end}}'"}</code></pre>
        A proxy on the host that reaches the published port <code>5000</code> also arrives
        from this network's gateway, so the same range covers it. Only requests from inside
        the range may name the visitor; anyone else's forwarded headers are ignored.
      </li>
      <li>
        Behind a Cloudflare Tunnel (<code>cloudflared</code>), also set
        <code>API_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=CF-Connecting-IP</code>: the tunnel is the origin's only way
        in, and Cloudflare puts the visitor's address there. Don't use it for the orange-cloud
        proxy in front of your own nginx or Caddy — nothing overwrites
        <code>CF-Connecting-IP</code> after Cloudflare, so anyone reaching the origin directly
        could send their own. There, keep the default <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, have the
        host proxy append the caller (nginx:
        <code>proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;</code>), and the
        API believes only that last hop. That hop is Cloudflare's edge unless the host proxy
        first restores the visitor from Cloudflare's own ranges (nginx's realip module:
        <code>set_real_ip_from</code> for each Cloudflare range,
        <code>real_ip_header CF-Connecting-IP;</code>). If Cloudflare should be the only way
        in, allow just Cloudflare's IP ranges at the origin's firewall.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep port <code>5000</code> off the internet: bind it to <code>127.0.0.1</code> or close
        it at the firewall, so the proxy is the only way in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Turn on HSTS at the proxy.</strong> The client sends its own
        <code>Content-Security-Policy</code>, <code>X-Content-Type-Options</code>,
        <code>Referrer-Policy</code> and <code>Permissions-Policy</code>, but it serves plain
        HTTP, so <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code> is the proxy's job (on Cloudflare:
        SSL/TLS → Edge Certificates → HSTS). Don't add a second
        <code>Content-Security-Policy</code> at the proxy — the browser enforces both, and
        anything only one of them allows is blocked.
      </li>
      <li>Recreate the stack:
        <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --force-recreate</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="The client only talks to the origins in .env">
      <p>
        Its content security policy is built when the <code>app</code> container starts,
        from <code>API_BASE_ADDRESS</code> and <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code>. If the browser
        reaches the API or Keycloak at any other address, it refuses the request — sign-in
        or sync fails with a "Refused to connect" error in the browser console. Fix the
        value and recreate <code>app</code>. Likewise the API accepts cross-origin calls
        only from <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code> — compose always passes it to the API, so
        leave it empty and every browser call to the API is refused.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <h3>Live updates</h3>
    <p>
      An open app holds one long-lived request, <code>GET /api/live</code>, a Server-Sent
      Events stream that tells it to sync the moment something changes. The headers and the 25-second heartbeat are chosen so stock proxy
      settings carry the stream.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Cloudflare (proxied DNS or Tunnel): expected to need no change; not tested
      here.</strong> Per its documented behaviour Cloudflare streams
      <code>text/event-stream</code> unbuffered and drops an origin connection silent for
      100 s (error 524); the 25 s heartbeat stays under that. Browsers talk HTTP/2
      to Cloudflare, so a stream does not use up the per-host connection limit. Only if a
      Cache Rule caches the API hostname, exclude <code>/api/live</code> (better: never cache
      the API).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>nginx in front of the API: stock settings were tested and need no change.</strong>
      Change only if the config has one of these:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>If your config has</th><th>Change it to</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>proxy_read_timeout</code> below 30s</td><td>≥ 60s, at least for <code>location /api/live</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>proxy_ignore_headers X-Accel-Buffering</code></td><td>remove it, or add <code>proxy_buffering off;</code> in <code>location /api/live</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>proxy_cache</code> on the API</td><td><code>proxy_cache off;</code> for <code>/api/live</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gzip_types</code> including <code>text/event-stream</code></td><td>remove it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>TLS without <code>http2</code> and no Cloudflare in front</td><td><code>http2 on;</code> — on HTTP/1.1 each visible tab holds one of the browser's six connections to the API (hidden tabs drop their stream); the default proxy-less <code>http://localhost:5000</code> is HTTP/1.1 too</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>An explicit block that does not rely on the response headers:</p>
    <pre><code>{`location /api/live {
    proxy_pass http://<api-upstream>;
    proxy_buffering off;
    proxy_cache off;
    proxy_read_timeout 1h;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      <strong>Caddy: expected to need no change; not tested here.</strong> Per its documented
      behaviour it flushes <code>text/event-stream</code> immediately.
    </p>
    <p>
      An integration test runs the stream through
      <code>nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.31.6-alpine</code> with a stock
      <code>proxy_pass</code> block and a short <code>proxy_read_timeout</code>. Cloudflare and
      Caddy are not tested.
    </p>

    <p>MongoDB publishes no port — it's reachable only from the other containers.</p>
    <p>
      None of the PSPad containers runs as root: the API runs as the .NET image's
      <code>app</code> user (UID 1654) and the client is served by unprivileged nginx
      (UID 101), both listening on <code>8080</code> inside their containers. The published
      ports above don't change.
    </p>

    <h2 id="backups">Backups</h2>
    <p>Three volumes hold everything:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>pspad_mongo-data</code> — areas, lists, tasks, the event log.</li>
      <li><code>pspad_mongo-keyfile</code> — the replica-set key. Lose it and <code>mongo-data</code> won't start.</li>
      <li><code>pspad_keycloak-data</code> — accounts and realm config.</li>
    </ul>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Never pass -v to docker compose down">
      <p>
        <code>down -v</code> deletes <code>pspad_keycloak-data</code>, and with it every
        account. PSPad keys its users by the Keycloak user's UUID, so accounts recreated
        afterwards are new people — the old areas, lists and tasks stay in MongoDB, owned by
        a user that can no longer sign in. <code>down</code> on its own is safe.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>Dump the database:</p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml exec -T mongo \
  sh -c 'mongodump --archive --username pspad --password "$MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD" --authenticationDatabase admin' \
  > pspad-backup.archive</code></pre>
    <p>
      Keep the single quotes — the password only exists inside the <code>mongo</code>
      container. Double quotes would expand it on your host instead, where it's empty.
    </p>

    <h2>Upgrading, restarting</h2>
    <pre><code>git pull
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
    <p>
      Volumes survive — only <code>down -v</code> destroys them. Give Mongo 10–30 seconds to
      report healthy again before assuming something's broken.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every image is pinned to an exact version, so an upgrade changes MongoDB, Keycloak
      or nginx only when <code>compose.yaml</code> or a <code>Dockerfile</code> in the
      release does.
    </p>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Upgrading from a release with floating images">
      <p>
        Older releases pulled <code>mongo:8</code> and Keycloak 26.0. This release pins
        MongoDB 8.3.11 and Keycloak 26.8.1, which migrates its embedded database on first
        start. Before you run <code>up</code>, take a <a href="#backups">database dump</a> and
        export the <strong>psplace</strong> realm. Nothing else changes: same
        <code>.env</code>, same ports, same volumes, and none of them needs new file
        permissions.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="database-user">Optional: give the API its own database user</h2>
    <p>
      Out of the box the API signs in to MongoDB as the root user <code>pspad</code>. To
      limit what a leaked connection string can do, create a user that can read and write
      <code>MONGO_DATABASE</code> and nothing else, using the example script
      <code>docker/mongo-app-user.js</code>. Run it as root inside the running
      <code>mongo</code> container (use your own database name and a password of letters,
      digits and <code>- . _ ~</code>):
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml cp docker/mongo-app-user.js mongo:/tmp/mongo-app-user.js
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml exec \
  -e MONGO_DATABASE=pspad -e MONGO_APP_USER=pspad-api -e MONGO_APP_PASSWORD='&lt;app password&gt;' \
  mongo sh -c 'mongosh --quiet -u pspad -p "$MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD" --authenticationDatabase admin /tmp/mongo-app-user.js'</code></pre>
    <p>
      It prints <code>created pspad-api on pspad</code>; running it again resets the password
      and roles. Then point the API at the new user with an override file next to
      <code>compose.yaml</code>, say <code>docker/compose.db-user.yaml</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>services:
  api:
    environment:
      Mongo__ConnectionString: mongodb://pspad-api:&lt;app password&gt;@mongo:27017/pspad?directConnection=true</code></pre>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml -f docker/compose.db-user.yaml up -d</code></pre>
    <p>
      Pass both files on every later <code>up</code>, or the API goes back to the root user.
      The root user stays for the healthcheck, the script and your backups. Running PSPad
      against your own MongoDB or Atlas? Give its user only <code>readWrite</code> on the
      PSPad database.
    </p>

    <h2>Two things worth knowing</h2>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Keycloak runs in development mode">
      <p>
        Its data lives in an embedded H2 database on a volume — fine for one user, but
        Keycloak doesn't support that mode for production, and a major image upgrade might
        not migrate cleanly. Export the realm before bumping it.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="The old keyfile is in the git history">
      <p>
        Early commits included <code>docker/mongo-keyfile</code>. Yours generates its own on
        first start and never uses that one. The old key only matters to someone who can
        already reach your MongoDB, which publishes no port.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Page>
